WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.430
I love making interesting videos.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:05.899
The most interesting topics are often exceptions—deviations
from the norm.

00:00:05.899 --> 00:00:11.910
All of us live in countries, where there are
laws and rules and governing bodies telling

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.820
us what we can and can’t do.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:19.250
But, 70% of the world is ocean, where there
are no countries—no governing bodies to

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.010
tell us what’s right and wrong.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.310
That’s why maritime law exists.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.789
Let’s start with a hypothetical: a baby
is born on a cruise ship sailing in the middle

00:00:27.789 --> 00:00:29.390
of the Atlantic ocean.

00:00:29.390 --> 00:00:31.890
What nationality does it take?

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:35.620
This is the coast of some fictional place
in some fictional country governed by some

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.200
fictional government.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.570
From this line, which is the water line at
the lowest low tide, every country is allowed

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:43.760
12 miles of territorial waters.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.170
It used to be 3 miles—the distance a cannon
could shoot off shore—but that has since

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.170
changed.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.330
Those twelve miles are the property of a country.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:56.330
They can do pretty much whatever they please
in it and all domestic laws apply.

00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:00.350
Foreign ships are, however, sometimes allowed
to enter into these waters under the principle

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.750
of innocent passage.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:05.940
If ships have an innocent purpose—which
does not include fishing, polluting, weapons

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.700
practice, or spying—they are allowed to
pass through territorial waters of a foreign

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:14.780
nation without permission as long as they
do so quickly and without stopping on shore.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:19.340
Beyond the territorial waters there is another
12 miles of the contiguous zone.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:23.990
This zone allows a country to enforce laws
as long as they fall into one of four categories.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:29.159
If the laws have to do with customs, taxation,
immigration, or pollution, they can be enforced

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:30.689
in the contiguous zone.

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:35.920
Beyond the contiguous waters is the Exclusive
Economic Zone, also known as the EEZ.

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.450
This zone extends 200 nautical miles from
shore.

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:44.090
Beyond the territorial waters the EEZ is in
international waters, however, only the country

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:48.310
who holds the exclusive economic zone has
the right to harvest natural resources in

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:49.310
this area.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.020
This law was originally set up to help with
disputes over fishing rights but has since

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.310
been incredibly useful with the boom in oil
drilling.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:02:01.479
All these laws do, however, occasionally cause
some disputes due to overlapping zones.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:04.590
This is the South China Sea—an incredibly
important waterway.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.750
Nearly 1/3rd of the world’s shipping traffic
passes through it and it reportedly has huge

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:11.340
untapped oil reserves.

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:15.980
China has this land so it says it has all
this water, Malaysia has this land so it says

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:21.270
it has all this water, Vietnam has this land
so it says it has all this water, Brunei has

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:25.240
this land so it says it has all this water,
the Philippines has this land so it says it

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.720
has all this water, and Taiwan has this land
so it says it has all this water.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.780
When two countries are less than 400 nautical
miles away from each other, it is up to them

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:34.230
to decide where their respective economic
zones end.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.020
Most solve it civilly by separating the zones
at the equidistant point from each of their

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:42.530
shores, however, when the stakes are so high,
such as in the South China Sea, countries

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.610
can be a bit less cordial.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.570
So, our cruise ship baby.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.340
Let’s change the hypothetical and say that
the cruise ship was sailing in US territorial

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.390
waters—less than 12 miles away from shore.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:56.820
Every oceangoing vessel is required to be
registered in some country.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:01.860
You’ll notice that most large cruise ships
are registered in tiny far-away countries.

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:07.400
Panama, a nation with fewer people that Minneapolis,
holds the registration of one quarter of the

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.810
world’s ships because taxes and labor costs
are low.

00:03:10.810 --> 00:03:15.100
When a ship is in international waters, the
laws of the country of registration apply.

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:19.510
A ship registered in Amsterdam could legally
have prostitution and marijuana on board,

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:23.640
as long as they got rid of the drugs and shut
down the brothels before sailing into territorial

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.830
waters.

00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:28.920
Once a ship is in the territorial waters of
a country, the onboard laws switch to that

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:31.080
of the country the ship is physically in.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.240
This is the same for nationality law, kinda.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:39.750
A baby born on a Dutch ship within 12 miles
of the US is a baby born in America.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.840
Since the US is one of the 30 countries that
unconditionally grants citizenship to any

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:48.230
baby born within the country, a baby born
in US territorial waters is lucky enough to

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:50.580
receive the world’s 8th most powerful passport.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:53.200
There are two exceptions to this rule.

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:57.550
Foreign Diplomats visiting or living in the
US with a diplomatic passport are not subject

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.720
to the laws of the US or any other nation
other than their own.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.510
Consequently, the babies of foreign diplomats
do not automatically receive American citizenship.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.710
Additionally, the babies of individuals staging
a hostile invasion or occupation of American

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:15.270
territory are not granted American citizenship
upon birth.

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:18.539
Here’s where things get even more confusing.

00:04:18.539 --> 00:04:22.180
Even though a ship in international waters
is an extension of the territory of the nation

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.990
it’s registered in law wise, the rules for
nationality are different.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.199
The United Nations Treaty on the Reduction
of Statelessness, which is followed by…

00:04:30.199 --> 00:04:34.080
some… countries, says that a baby born in
international waters should just take the

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.069
nationality of their parents.

00:04:36.069 --> 00:04:39.880
Most of the world’s countries use the principle
of bloodline to determine if a baby should

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.729
get citizenship rather than whether or not
a baby was born in the country.

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:48.080
However, there are some countries that won’t
give citizenship to a baby born outside the

00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.080
country.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:53.180
In that case, the baby would take the citizenship
of the country in which the ship was registered.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:55.070
Alright, that’s enough with babies.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:58.180
There’s a long history of exploiting maritime
laws.

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:02.870
During prohibition, US ships started to change
their registration to Panama and other foreign

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.699
countries so they could serve alcohol in international
waters.

00:05:06.699 --> 00:05:10.729
In the mid-century, casino boats left from
many cities where gambling was illegal to

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:13.699
partake in legal gambling in international
waters.

00:05:13.699 --> 00:05:19.380
In 2005, entrepreneur Roger Green started
SeaCode, a company that planned to evade US

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.960
labor laws by placing an old cruise ship 12
miles off the shore of California.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:27.300
They would bring in foreign coders and house
them in this ship where they would not have

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:31.979
to abide by US wage laws or go through the
difficult visa application process.

00:05:31.979 --> 00:05:37.099
The idea never came to fruition but the technical
legality of it just shows how convoluted maritime

00:05:37.099 --> 00:05:38.099
law is.

00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:40.800
The laws for airplanes are pretty much the
same.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:45.830
Technically, once an airplane has taken off,
the laws of the country of registration apply.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:49.680
The only law that is applies and differs among
countries is the drinking age.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.599
A British Airways flight from New York to
London can serve alcohol to 18 year olds,

00:05:54.599 --> 00:05:59.349
however, in most cases, airlines choose to
follow the laws of the origin country.

00:05:59.349 --> 00:06:04.099
Spacecraft also follow very similar laws,
and luckily, I have a whole other video just

00:06:04.099 --> 00:06:05.530
about Space Law.

00:06:05.530 --> 00:06:06.809
Make sure to check it out here.

